<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.2.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00724</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.2.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9d653e40-cc19-496f-993a-66821d1d3495</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Use graphing technology to create a scatter plot and find the best fit line.</para>

        <exercise id="_2213c4f6-63a8-43f1-88d4-65cbbcb95f2b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">What does the best fit line estimate for the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-value when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 100?</para>
      <media alt="">
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>

            <table class="os-raise-skinnytable unnumbered" id="table-00001">
              <tgroup cols="2">
                <thead>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <row>
                    <entry> 83 </entry>
                    <entry> 102 </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry> 87 </entry>
                    <entry> 115 </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry> 91 </entry>
                    <entry> 107 </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry> 93 </entry>
                    <entry> 122 </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry> 97 </entry>
                    <entry> 125 </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry> 97 </entry>
                    <entry> 127 </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry> 101 </entry>
                    <entry> 120 </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry> 104 </entry>
                    <entry> 127 </entry>
                  </row>
                </tbody>
              </tgroup>
            </table>

            <para id="para-00004">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00005">100</para>
            <para id="para-00006">125</para>
            <para id="para-00007">150</para>
            <para id="para-00008">120</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00009">Correct! The line of best fit is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1.089</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15.588</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, so when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 100, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
              is about 125.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">Try again. Revisit activities 3.2.2 – 3.23.</para>
            <para id="para-00011">The correct answer for the line of best fit is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1.089</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15.588</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, so when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
              is 100, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is about 125. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="c769c078-adf6-4f2f-bd88-1986cf4d4423" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00012">A seed is planted in a glass pot, and its height is measured in centimeters every
              day.</para>
            <media alt="A scatter plot. Horizontal, 0 to 30, by 1’s, labeled days. Vertical, negative 5 to 3, by point 5’s, labeled height in centimeters. 21 dots trend linearly up and to the right with a line a best fit.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_04_13_01.svg"/>
            </media>
            <para id="para-00013">The best fit line is given by the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.404</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>5.18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
              represents the height of the plant above ground level, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number
              of days since it was planted.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">What is the slope of the best fit line? What does the slope of the line mean in
              this situation?</para>
            <para id="para-00015">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00016">The slope is 5.18, which means for every day, the plant grows 5.18 centimeters.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">The slope is 0.404, which means for every day, the plant grows 0.404 centimeters.</para>
            <para id="para-00018">The slope is -5.18, which means for every day, the plant shrinks -5.18
              centimeters.</para>
            <para id="para-00019">The slope is 0.404, which means the plant grows 1 centimeter every 0.404 days.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00020">Correct! The slope is 0.404, which means for every day, the plant grows 0.404
              centimeters.</para>
            <para id="para-00021">Try again. Revisit activities 3.2.2 – 3.2.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00022">The correct answer is: The slope is 0.404, which means for every day, the plant
              grows 0.404 centimeters.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <para id="para-00023">Use the following information to answer questions 3 - 5.</para>
      <para id="para-00024">
        <quote id="quote-00001">The scatter plots below show different lines that students used to model the
        relationship between body mass (in pounds) and bite force (in pounds) for crocodiles.</quote>
        <quote id="quote-00002">Match
        each graph to one of the equations below, and explain your reasoning. Let \(B\) represent
        bite force (in pounds) and \(W\) represent body mass (in pounds).</quote>
        <quote id="quote-00003">
          <media alt="">
            <image mime-type="" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_06_04.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </quote>
      </para>

        <exercise id="_2a497ced-dbcd-445e-ac8d-01255b47f460" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00025">Which graph represents data that could be modeled by the equation, \(B = 3.28W +
              126\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00026">Correct! Line 3 and Equation 1 match because the intercept of Equation 1 is the
              smallest, which seems to match the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00027">Try again. Revisit activity 3.2.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00028">The correct answer is: Line 3 and Equation 1 match because the intercept of
              Equation 1 is the smallest, which seems to match the graph.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7c1ce9c3-b22a-497d-b5f0-292bb5e4b48a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00029">Which graph represents data that could be modeled by the equation, \(B = 3.04W +
              351\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00030">Correct! Line 2 and Equation 2 match because the intercept of Equation 2 is
              larger, so it matches Line 2, which has a <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept closer to 400.</para>
            <para id="para-00031">Try again. Revisit activity 3.2.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00032">The correct answer is: Line 2 and Equation 2 match because the intercept of
              Equation 2 is larger, so it matches Line 2, which has a <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept closer to 400.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_970607b5-89fc-4744-8890-a013a6d9f3fa" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00033">Which graph represents data that could be modeled by the equation, \(B = 2.16W +
              267\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00034">Correct! Line 1 and Equation 3 match because the intercept of 267 appears to match
              the graph, which has the second-largest intercept.</para>
            <para id="para-00035">Try again. Revisit activity 3.2.1.</para>
            <para id="para-00036">The correct answer is: Line 1 and Equation 3 match because the intercept of 267
              appears to match the graph, which has the second-largest intercept.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f2e32e15-3966-494b-b130-e03c979a9bfe" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00037">Mike owns a grass cutting business. The table below shows how many yards, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>,
              he can cut in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> days.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable unnumbered" id="table-00002">
              <tgroup cols="5">
                <tbody>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Number of days, x</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>1</entry>
                    <entry>2</entry>
                    <entry>3</entry>
                    <entry>4</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Number of yards cut, Y</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>6</entry>
                    <entry>12</entry>
                    <entry>18</entry>
                    <entry>24</entry>
                  </row>
                </tbody>
              </tgroup>
            </table>

            <para id="para-00038">Which of the following equations is a linear model representing the number of
              yards, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, Mike can cut in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> days.</para>
            <para id="para-00039">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00040"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00041"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00042"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00043"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00044">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00045">Since the change in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 6 and change in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 1, the slope is 6. Working
              backwards, when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> so the equation becomes <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00046">Try again. Revisit activity 3.2.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00047">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
